course: dictionary
lesson: 5658
title: Literal or not?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Literal means taking words in their most basic, exact meaning. Some phrases do not mean what their words say. «It's raining cats and dogs» does not mean animals are falling from the sky. Its figurative meaning is that it is raining very heavily.
explain: Read carefully to tell the difference. «I'm starving» after school lunch is hyperbole, a dramatic way of saying you are hungry. But in a history book about a famine, where people had no food for weeks, starving is literal: it is true. The same word can be either.
explain: Literal has other uses too. A literal translation copies each word across from one language to another, which can sound strange. In maths, read every question literally and exactly, because every word can change the answer.
fact: Literal means taking words in their most basic, exact meaning.
fact: A figure of speech is a phrase that means more than its words say, like «raining cats and dogs».
fact: Literal can also mean word for word, as in a literal translation.
fact: Informal use: literal can mean real and not exaggerated, as in «the literal truth».
fact: Hyperbole is a deliberate exaggeration, like «I've told you a million times».
exercise: «It's raining cats and dogs.» What does this mean literally? | It is cold and windy outside | *It is raining very heavily | Animals are falling from the sky | A dog and a cat are running in the rain
exercise: Lily says «I'm starving» after lunch. Is she using starving literally? | Yes, she has had no food for weeks | No, starving is a colour | Yes, the school has run out of food | *No, it is hyperbole, a dramatic way of saying she is hungry
exercise: What does it mean to take something literally? | *To understand the words in their exact, basic meaning | To guess what someone feels | To laugh at a joke | To translate a poem into rhyme
test: In a history book about a famine, «starving» is literal because | It is a figure of speech | It means the same as hungry after lunch | *People had no food for weeks | People were at a party
test: A literal translation means | The words are sung like a song | The story is made up | *Each word is copied across, word for word | It is shorter than the original
exam: Which sentence uses a figure of speech? | The class read the book aloud | The water in the river is cold | The famine left people with no food for weeks | *«It's raining cats and dogs»
